. LESSON 11 ADVANCED CONCEPTS
. In these examples color 1 is teal, not red. $L1 says which of the
. palette's 256 colors color 1 shows (lesson 7); 29 is teal, and
. $L1=1 makes it red again.
$L1=29
. A macro can be used as a function, and RETURN gives its value.
SAM=[a=10
b=20
PRINT SQUAREM(?a,?b)]
SQUAREM=[INPUT a,b
RETURN a*a+b*b]
SAM

. .B runs a macro in the background, over and over, while you go on
. typing. Type CHANGE.B to steer colors 0 to 2 with the joystick, and
. CTRL+C to stop every macro. The lesson's NB.B uses the system macro
. NB, which this emulator doesn't have.
CHANGE=[$L0=$L0+$X1*8
$L1=$L1+$Y1*8
$L2=$K1]

. .F runs a macro in the foreground, every frame or, after TIMEOUT n,
. every n frames: here a box in XOR every 5 seconds.
TIMEDBOX=[TIMEOUT 300
BOX 0,0,200,200,7]
COMPILE TIMEDBOX,CTIME

. ONERROR jumps to a label when an error happens.
UGH=[ARRAY SAM,10
A=-1
ONERROR 1OUT
PR SAM(A=A+1);SKIP 0
1OUT PRINT "SAM OUT OF BOUNDS"]
UGH

. THE ANIMATION, three macros at once. FACE draws a clock face and ends.
. HANDS.B moves the clock's hands with the time, the second hand one
. degree every 10 frames, and ORBIT.B sends a moon round the clock,
. both in the background, while CTIME.F flips the middle of the screen
. every 5 seconds. Everything moving is drawn in XOR, so the flips and
. the hands never spoil each other. The prompt stays free: type while
. they run. CTRL+C stops them all.
FACE=[CLEAR
CIRCLE 0,0,96,80,3
N=0
LINE SIN(N*30)*90,COS(N*30)*75,4;LINE SIN(N*30)*80,COS(N*30)*67,3
IF (N=N+1)<12,SKIP -1]
HANDS=[S=$SC*6;M=$MN*6
LINE 0,0,4;LINE SIN(M)*60,COS(M)*50,6
LINE 0,0,4;LINE SIN(S)*78,COS(S)*65,7
1TICK WAIT 10
LINE 0,0,4;LINE SIN(S+1)*78,COS(S+1)*65,7;LINE 0,0,4;LINE SIN(S)*78,COS(S)*65,7;S=S+1
IF S\360==0,LINE 0,0,4;LINE SIN(M+6)*60,COS(M+6)*50,6;LINE 0,0,4;LINE SIN(M)*60,COS(M)*50,6;M=M+6
GOTO 1TICK]
ORBIT=[A=0;F=0
1GO X=SIN(A)*132;Y=COS(A)*90;BOX X,Y,5,5,5;IF F,BOX OX,OY,5,5,5
OX=X;OY=Y;F=1;A=A+4;WAIT 1;GOTO 1GO]
FACE
HANDS.B
ORBIT.B
CTIME.F
